claims.claim:
sd_notify without extra dependencies: systemd documents a self-contained Python implementation of the notify protocol under the MIT-0 licence. It is about 10 lines of stdlib code:
- read $NOTIFY_SOCKET;
- '@' means an abstract socket, so replace it with '\0';
- send one datagram on socket(AF_UNIX, SOCK_DGRAM | SOCK_CLOEXEC);
- payload 'READY=1', 'WATCHDOG=1', 'STOPPING=1', etc.
The protocol is declared stable, so neither the systemd-python nor the sdnotify package is needed.

Design recommendation (my reasoning, not from systemd docs): send WATCHDOG=1 from an asyncio task on the main event loop, not from a thread. Send it only when the control loop has recently completed a tick and a fresh sensor read (or deliberately recorded an invalid one). A pinging thread keeps systemd happy while the event loop is blocked, which defeats hang detection.

Raspberry Pi OS Lite 2026-10-06 (Trixie) preinstalls, on BOTH armhf and arm64:
- python3 3.13.5 and python3-venv;
- python3-gpiozero 2.0.1-0+rpt1+trixie, python3-lgpio 0.2.2-1~rpt1+trixie and python3-libgpiod 2.2.1;
- polkitd 126 and sudo 1.9.16p2;
- the signature tools gpgv 2.4.7, sqv 1.3.0 and openssl 3.5.7, plus python3-cryptography 43.0.0.

Consequences for the update design:
- A venv created with --system-site-packages can take gpiozero and lgpio from apt. Those are RPi-patched, built for the system Python, and upgraded together with it.
- The release wheel can then be pure Python (py3-none-any): one artifact for armhf, arm64 and developer PCs, with no native wheels to build.
- A root updater can verify OpenPGP or Ed25519 signatures with tools already on the image.
Not checked: the Bookworm (legacy) images.
